{"step_index":9278,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T06:32:50Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-26T06:32:50Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-26T06:32:50Z\nFile Path: `file:///home/azureuser/.webterminal/media/media_1790404360084_2787.md`\nTotal Lines: 103\nTotal Bytes: 5901\nShowing lines 1 to 103\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # ==============================================================================\n2: # /etc/sudoers.d/99-azureuser-restricted (DRAFT ONLY)\n3: # ==============================================================================\n4: # Principle of Least Privilege: Whitelist-only with NOEXEC\n5: # Target: Azure VM 'FatehAli' (User: azureuser)\n6: # Status: DRAFT - DO NOT DEPLOY WITHOUT EXPLICIT APPROVAL AND BACKUP\n7: #\n8: # CHANGE LOG (fixed from previous draft):\n9: # - FIXED: tag syntax was `NOEXEC: NOPASSWD:` (invalid — space-separated tags\n10: #   before the alias list are not parsed the way the old draft assumed).\n11: #   Correct sudoers syntax chains tags with colons, no spaces, immediately\n12: #   before the command list: NOPASSWD:NOEXEC:\n13: #\n14: # CRITICAL DEPLOYMENT PREREQUISITE:\n15: # 1. The default file '/etc/sudoers.d/90-cloud-init-users' currently grants:\n16: #    'azureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL'\n17: #    If 90-cloud-init-users remains active, this restricted policy will be\n18: #    bypassed because sudo evaluates rules additively and the LAST matching\n19: #    rule for a given command wins — but a broad NOPASSWD:ALL earlier in\n20: #    evaluation order can still grant access this file tries to deny.\n21: #    To activate, 90-cloud-init-users MUST be removed or renamed with a\n22: #    lower priority prefix (e.g. 00-) so this file's rules are evaluated after it,\n23: #    or simply deleted since NOPASSWD:ALL is being replaced entirely.\n24: # 2. Safety Rule: Always keep an active Azure Serial Console session open, or\n25: #    take an Azure VM Disk Snapshot before modifying sudoers!\n26: # 3. Always validate syntax before installing:\n27: #      sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/99-azureuser-restricted\n28: #    A non-zero exit code means DO NOT proceed — fix the syntax first.\n29: # 4. After installing, open a SECOND ssh session (do not close your current\n30: #    one) and test `sudo -l` as azureuser before logging out of the first.\n31: #    If sudo is broken, your current session's sudo cache may still work\n32: #    long enough to revert via the serial console.\n33: # ==============================================================================\n34: \n35: # Defaults specification\n36: Defaults    env_reset\n37: Defaults    mail_badpass\n38: Defaults    secure_path=\"/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin\"\n39: \n40: # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n41: # 1. COMMAND ALIASES (STRICT WHITELIST ONLY)\n42: # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n43: \n44: # Package management (apt / apt-get)\n45: # Restricted to update, upgrade, and install.\n46: # NOEXEC below prevents pager/GTFOBins escapes (e.g. less/vi/sh spawned\n47: # from within apt's package-configuration scripts or a pager).\n48: Cmnd_Alias APT_CMNDS = /usr/bin/apt update, \\\n49:                        /usr/bin/apt-get update, \\\n50:                        /usr/bin/apt upgrade, \\\n51:                        /usr/bin/apt-get upgrade, \\\n52:                        /usr/bin/apt install *, \\\n53:                        /usr/bin/apt-get install *, \\\n54:                        /usr/bin/apt autoremove, \\\n55:                        /usr/bin/apt-get autoremove\n56: \n57: # Service management (systemctl)\n58: # Restricted to restart, start, stop, reload, and status of units.\n59: # Deliberately EXCLUDED: systemctl edit (opens $EDITOR as root — GTFOBins),\n60: # systemctl daemon-reexec, systemctl run (arbitrary command as a transient unit).\n61: Cmnd_Alias SYSTEMCTL_CMNDS = /usr/bin/systemctl start *, \\\n62:                              /usr/bin/systemctl stop *, \\\n63:                              /usr/bin/systemctl restart *, \\\n64:                              /usr/bin/systemctl reload *, \\\n65:                              /usr/bin/systemctl status *\n66: \n67: # Container / Process management (if required for Alco / ERPNext)\n68: Cmnd_Alias DOCKER_CMNDS = /usr/bin/docker compose *\n69: \n70: # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n71: # 2. ACCESS SPECIFICATION WITH NOEXEC (CORRECTED SYNTAX)\n72: # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n73: # NOEXEC prevents the executed command from spawning further processes via\n74: # execve() (e.g. a shell escape from inside a pager or an apt maintainer\n75: # script). Tags are chained with colons immediately before the command list,\n76: # NOT separated by spaces from the alias — that was the bug in the prior draft.\n77: #\n78: # NOTE: NOEXEC relies on LD_PRELOAD interposition inside sudo and does NOT\n79: # work on statically-linked binaries, and has known limited effect on some\n80: # newer glibc/systemd-related binaries. Treat NOEXEC as defense-in-depth,\n81: # not an absolute guarantee — the primary defense is still that dangerous\n82: # binaries (chattr, rm, dd, bash, python, etc.) are never in this whitelist.\n83: \n84: azureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: APT_CMNDS\n85: azureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: SYSTEMCTL_CMNDS\n86: azureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: DOCKER_CMNDS\n87: \n88: # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n89: # 3. EXPLICIT EXCLUSION SUMMARY (DENY-BY-DEFAULT)\n90: # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n91: # In sudoers, any command not explicitly matched above is DENIED by default.\n92: # The following dangerous administrative binaries are strictly EXCLUDED\n93: # (listed here for audit clarity only — they do not need explicit \"!\" deny\n94: # rules because they were never granted):\n95: # - /usr/bin/chattr, /usr/bin/lsattr (cannot modify or inspect immutability)\n96: # - /bin/rm, /usr/bin/rm, /usr/bin/unlink, /usr/bin/find (cannot delete via root)\n97: # - /bin/dd, /sbin/fdisk, /sbin/mkfs* (cannot overwrite raw block devices)\n98: # - /bin/mount, /bin/umount (cannot remount filesystems read-write/exec)\n99: # - /usr/bin/visudo, /bin/su, /usr/bin/pkexec (cannot modify sudoers or spawn root)\n100: # - /bin/bash, /bin/sh, /usr/bin/python*, /usr/bin/perl (cannot spawn root shells)\n101: # - systemctl edit, apt-config, dpkg-reconfigure (known GTFOBins root-shell vectors)\n102: # ==============================================================================\n103: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
