{"step_index":9280,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T06:32:54Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-26T06:32:54Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-26T06:32:54Z\nFile Path: `file:///home/azureuser/.webterminal/media/media_1790404360504_2788.md`\nTotal Lines: 132\nTotal Bytes: 7558\nShowing lines 1 to 132\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # PARENT DIRECTORY LOCK SPECIFICATION & ARCHITECTURAL COMPARISON\n2: \n3: Target Directory: `/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER`\n4: Target Host: Azure VM `FatehAli`\n5: Status: DRAFT / ARCHITECTURAL ANALYSIS (NO ACTIONS EXECUTED)\n6: \n7: CHANGE LOG (fixed from previous draft):\n8: - CORRECTED a claim in the original Option B analysis: setting `+i` on\n9:   `AGY-MASTER` itself does NOT selectively allow writes only to files, while\n10:   blocking directory rename. In ext4, `+i` on a directory blocks ALL new\n11:   directory-entry operations inside it — creating, deleting, or renaming\n12:   any file or subdirectory directly under it. Existing subdirectories that\n13:   do NOT themselves have `+i` remain writable **inside themselves** (you can\n14:   still edit a file's contents inside `TASKS/active/`), but you cannot add or\n15:   remove entries in `AGY-MASTER/` itself once you've locked it.\n16: - This means: every subdirectory AGY needs to write NEW files into (queue\n17:   folders, logs, reports) MUST be created BEFORE `chattr +i` is applied to\n18:   the parent, and none of those specific subdirectories should themselves\n19:   get `+i` (only the parent + selected top-level files should).\n20: - Execution order across drafts 02 and 03 has been made explicit — see\n21:   Section 4.\n22: \n23: ---\n24: \n25: ## 1. THE PROBLEM: EXT4 DIRECTORY RENAMING VULNERABILITY\n26: \n27: In Linux (POSIX / ext4), the permission to rename or delete a directory\n28: depends on the write permission of its **PARENT directory**, not the\n29: directory itself.\n30: \n31: - Current Parent Directory: `/home/azureuser` (Owned by `azureuser:azureuser`,\n32:   permission `drwxr-xr-x`)\n33: - Because `azureuser` has write permission in `/home/azureuser`, any process\n34:   running as `azureuser` can execute:\n35:   ```bash\n36:   mv /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER-DELETED\n37:   ```\n38:   even if the files inside have individual `+i` protections.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 2. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS: OPTION A vs. OPTION B\n43: \n44: | Dimension | Option A: Ownership `root:root` + Mode `755` | Option B: Directory-level `chattr +i` |\n45: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n46: | **Mechanism** | Standard POSIX DAC (Discretionary Access Control) | Linux ext4 Inode Immutable Flag (`FS_IMMUTABLE_FL`) |\n47: | **Can azureuser rename `AGY-MASTER`?** | **NO** — once `azureuser` loses write on the *parent* (`/home/azureuser` would need root ownership too, which is impractical) — see caveat below. | **NO (blocked by ext4 driver itself)**. An immutable directory cannot be renamed, moved, or have entries added/removed, regardless of parent permissions. |\n48: | **Caveat** | Actually requires changing permissions on `/home/azureuser` too (the true parent), which affects azureuser's own home directory usability broadly. Changing only `AGY-MASTER`'s own ownership to root:root does **not** stop rename — rename permission is governed by the *containing* directory (`/home/azureuser`), not the directory being renamed. | No caveat — this correctly targets the actual vulnerable object. |\n49: | **Can azureuser create NEW files/subdirs directly in `AGY-MASTER/`?** | No (root-owned, mode 755, no write bit for azureuser). | No (kernel immutability blocks any new directory entry). |\n50: | **Can azureuser write inside EXISTING subdirectories (e.g. `TASKS/active/`)?** | Yes, if those subdirectories are still owned by azureuser. | Yes, if those subdirectories do not themselves have `+i`. |\n51: | **Resilience against sudo escalation** | If azureuser regains any sudo/root path, `chown` reverses this instantly. | If `chattr` is excluded from sudoers (draft 01), azureuser **cannot** remove `+i` even with a compromised process, because removing it requires `CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE` which only root has and sudo won't grant. |\n52: \n53: ### Correction to the original draft's Option A row:\n54: The original comparison implied Option A only requires changing `AGY-MASTER`'s\n55: own ownership. That is incorrect — rename/delete permission comes from the\n56: **parent** directory (`/home/azureuser`), so Option A would actually require\n57: locking down `/home/azureuser` itself, which is invasive and affects\n58: azureuser's whole home directory. **This is why Option B is the practical\n59: recommendation** — it acts directly on `AGY-MASTER`, not its parent.\n60: \n61: ---\n62: \n63: ## 3. ARCHITECTURAL RECOMMENDATION: OPTION B (Directory `+i`, Pre-Created Subdirectories)\n64: \n65: ### Why Option B is superior:\n66: 1. `chattr +i` on `/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER` completely solves the rename\n67:    vulnerability: the ext4 kernel driver refuses to rename, move, or delete\n68:    `AGY-MASTER`, and refuses to add/remove entries in it directly — regardless\n69:    of `/home/azureuser`'s own permissions.\n70: 2. It requires no change to `/home/azureuser` itself, so azureuser's broader\n71:    home-directory usability is untouched.\n72: 3. Combined with draft 01 (chattr removed from sudoers), azureuser has no path\n73:    to reverse this — not via sudo, not via direct syscall (`ioctl` with\n74:    `FS_IOC_SETFLAGS` requires `CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE`).\n75: \n76: ### The operational requirement this creates:\n77: Because locking `AGY-MASTER` blocks new entries directly inside it, **every\n78: subdirectory AGY will ever need to write new files into must already exist\n79: before you apply `+i`**. This is why draft 03's script has been reordered —\n80: see Section 4.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 4. DRAFT COMMAND SPECIFICATION (FOR HUMAN REVIEW — DO NOT EXECUTE AUTONOMOUSLY)\n85: \n86: ### Correct execution order (this matters — reversing steps 1 and 3 below\n87: ### will lock AGY out of its own working directories):\n88: \n89: ```bash\n90: # STEP 1 — Create ALL runtime state subdirectories AGY will ever write\n91: # new files into, BEFORE locking the parent. Add any other subdirectory\n92: # AGY's code creates at runtime to this list now — anything missed here\n93: # will be impossible to create later without temporarily un-immutabilizing\n94: # the parent (which requires sudo access to chattr, which draft 01 removes).\n95: mkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/TASKS/{queue,active,completed}\n96: mkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/INCIDENTS/{active,resolved}\n97: mkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/ACTIONS/{pending,approved,history}\n98: mkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/REPORTS/daily\n99: mkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS\n100: chown -R azureuser:azureuser \\\n101:     /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/TASKS \\\n102:     /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/INCIDENTS \\\n103:     /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/ACTIONS \\\n104:     /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/REPORTS \\\n105:     /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS\n106: \n107: # STEP 2 — Lock top-level canonical architecture files individually\n108: sudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/AGENTS.md\n109: sudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/PROJECT_REGISTRY.md\n110: sudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/safety_levels.json\n111: sudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/protected_registry.json\n112: \n113: # STEP 3 — LAST: lock the AGY-MASTER directory itself.\n114: # Run this only after Steps 1 and 2 are fully verified complete — once this\n115: # runs, no new file or subdirectory can be added directly under AGY-MASTER\n116: # (existing subdirectories from Step 1 remain writable inside themselves).\n117: sudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER\n118: ```\n119: \n120: ### Rollback Procedure (If Maintenance Required):\n121: This MUST be run by a human with direct console/SSH access — it is not\n122: available to azureuser via sudo once draft 01 is applied, by design. It\n123: requires either temporarily re-adding a scoped `chattr` sudo rule, or logging\n124: in as root directly via Azure Serial Console.\n125: ```bash\n126: sudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER\n127: sudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/AGENTS.md\n128: sudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/PROJECT_REGISTRY.md\n129: sudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/safety_levels.json\n130: sudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/protected_registry.json\n131: ```\n132: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
