{"step_index":9287,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T06:33:06Z","thinking":"The initial file's syntax for restricted commands within the sudoers file was incorrect; tags were concatenated without spaces. Corrected this, ensuring each command set (APT, SYSTEMCTL, DOCKER) is listed on a new line with the proper `NOPASSWD:NOEXEC:` syntax. The objective is to refine the configuration of the commands.\n\n\nExamining the mechanism of `NOEXEC`, its implications for dynamic linking and static binaries were considered to ensure complete restriction. The configuration must disable `90-cloud-init-users` to avoid permission conflicts. A secondary SSH session is recommended for testing.\n\nThe initial locking strategy was flawed. Changing ownership of the master directory doesn't prevent renaming; the parent's permissions control that. The right approach involves locking the master directory after subdirectories are established and top-level files made immutable.\n\n\nRevised the backup script to ensure commits are pushed to the remote repository and included deploy key instructions for SSH access. Documentation clarified the method of data ingestion into the object storage system without VM credentials.\n\nVerification tests are enhanced: exit codes are now the primary check; a dedicated test uses a syscall to probe restrictions directly, and checks confirm the `i` attribute for immutability. Included a sanity check to verify subdirectory writability even after locking the parent. An IMDS token test was added to check access.\n\n\nThe peer-reviewed files include comprehensive tests and corrections for edge cases, addressing ordering issues and replacing weak assertions with robust tests. The backup script has been improved to ensure proper external pushing, and sudoers syntax is corrected for `NOEXEC` chaining. The immutability order of execution is clarified. Verification confirms the improvements should be synchronized with the local drafts.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"# ==============================================================================\n# /etc/sudoers.d/99-azureuser-restricted (DRAFT ONLY)\n# ==============================================================================\n# Principle of Least Privilege: Whitelist-only with NOEXEC\n# Target: Azure VM 'FatehAli' (User: azureuser)\n# Status: DRAFT - DO NOT DEPLOY WITHOUT EXPLICIT APPROVAL AND BACKUP\n#\n# CHANGE LOG (fixed from previous draft):\n# - FIXED: tag syntax was `NOEXEC: NOPASSWD:` (invalid — space-separated tags\n#   before the alias list are not parsed the way the old draft assumed).\n#   Correct sudoers syntax chains tags with colons, no spaces, immediately\n#   before the command list: NOPASSWD:NOEXEC:\n#\n# CRITICAL DEPLOYMENT PREREQUISITE:\n# 1. The default file '/etc/sudoers.d/90-cloud-init-users' currently grants:\n#    'azureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL'\n#    If 90-cloud-init-users remains active, this restricted policy will be\n#    bypassed because sudo evaluates rules additively and the LAST matching\n#    rule for a given command wins — but a broad NOPASSWD:ALL earlier in\n#    evaluation order can still grant access this file tries to deny.\n#    To activate, 90-cloud-init-users MUST be removed or renamed with a\n#    lower priority prefix (e.g. 00-) so this file's rules are evaluated after it,\n#    or simply deleted since NOPASSWD:ALL is being replaced entirely.\n# 2. Safety Rule: Always keep an active Azure Serial Console session open, or\n#    take an Azure VM Disk Snapshot before modifying sudoers!\n# 3. Always validate syntax before installing:\n#      sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/99-azureuser-restricted\n#    A non-zero exit code means DO NOT proceed — fix the syntax first.\n# 4. After installing, open a SECOND ssh session (do not close your current\n#    one) and test `sudo -l` as azureuser before logging out of the first.\n#    If sudo is broken, your current session's sudo cache may still work\n#    long enough to revert via the serial console.\n# ==============================================================================\n\n# Defaults specification\nDefaults    env_reset\nDefaults    mail_badpass\nDefaults    secure_path=\"/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin\"\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 1. COMMAND ALIASES (STRICT WHITELIST ONLY)\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n# Package management (apt / apt-get)\n# Restricted to update, upgrade, and install.\n# NOEXEC below prevents pager/GTFOBins escapes (e.g. less/vi/sh spawned\n# from within apt's package-configuration scripts or a pager).\nCmnd_Alias APT_CMNDS = /usr/bin/apt update, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt-get update, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt upgrade, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt-get upgrade, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt install *, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt-get install *, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt autoremove, \\\n                       /usr/bin/apt-get autoremove\n\n# Service management (systemctl)\n# Restricted to restart, start, stop, reload, and status of units.\n# Deliberately EXCLUDED: systemctl edit (opens $EDITOR as root — GTFOBins),\n# systemctl daemon-reexec, systemctl run (arbitrary command as a transient unit).\nCmnd_Alias SYSTEMCTL_CMNDS = /usr/bin/systemctl start *, \\\n                             /usr/bin/systemctl stop *, \\\n                             /usr/bin/systemctl restart *, \\\n                             /usr/bin/systemctl reload *, \\\n                             /usr/bin/systemctl status *\n\n# Container / Process management (if required for Alco / ERPNext)\nCmnd_Alias DOCKER_CMNDS = /usr/bin/docker compose *\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 2. ACCESS SPECIFICATION WITH NOEXEC (CORRECTED SYNTAX)\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# NOEXEC prevents the executed command from spawning further processes via\n# execve() (e.g. a shell escape from inside a pager or an apt maintainer\n# script). Tags are chained with colons immediately before the command list,\n# NOT separated by spaces from the alias — that was the bug in the prior draft.\n#\n# NOTE: NOEXEC relies on LD_PRELOAD interposition inside sudo and does NOT\n# work on statically-linked binaries, and has known limited effect on some\n# newer glibc/systemd-related binaries. Treat NOEXEC as defense-in-depth,\n# not an absolute guarantee — the primary defense is still that dangerous\n# binaries (chattr, rm, dd, bash, python, etc.) are never in this whitelist.\n\nazureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: APT_CMNDS\nazureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: SYSTEMCTL_CMNDS\nazureuser ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: DOCKER_CMNDS\n\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# 3. EXPLICIT EXCLUSION SUMMARY (DENY-BY-DEFAULT)\n# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# In sudoers, any command not explicitly matched above is DENIED by default.\n# The following dangerous administrative binaries are strictly EXCLUDED\n# (listed here for audit clarity only — they do not need explicit \"!\" deny\n# rules because they were never granted):\n# - /usr/bin/chattr, /usr/bin/lsattr (cannot modify or inspect immutability)\n# - /bin/rm, /usr/bin/rm, /usr/bin/unlink, /usr/bin/find (cannot delete via root)\n# - /bin/dd, /sbin/fdisk, /sbin/mkfs* (cannot overwrite raw block devices)\n# - /bin/mount, /bin/umount (cannot remount filesystems read-write/exec)\n# - /usr/bin/visudo, /bin/su, /usr/bin/pkexec (cannot modify sudoers or spawn root)\n# - /bin/bash, /bin/sh, /usr/bin/python*, /usr/bin/perl (cannot spawn root shells)\n# - systemctl edit, apt-config, dpkg-reconfigure (known GTFOBins root-shell vectors)\n# ==============================================================================\n","Description":"Update 01-sudoers-restricted.draft with chained NOPASSWD:NOEXEC: tag syntax and refined exclusions","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS/01-sudoers-restricted.draft","toolAction":"Updating 01-sudoers-restricted.draft","toolSummary":"Sync 01-sudoers-restricted"}}]}
{"step_index":9288,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T06:33:18Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-26T06:33:18Z\nCompleted At: 2026-09-26T06:33:18Z\nCreated file file:///home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS/01-sudoers-restricted.draft with requested content.\nIf relevant, proactively run terminal commands to execute this code for the USER. Don't ask for permission."}
{"step_index":9289,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-26T06:33:18Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"# PARENT DIRECTORY LOCK SPECIFICATION & ARCHITECTURAL COMPARISON\n\nTarget Directory: `/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER`\nTarget Host: Azure VM `FatehAli`\nStatus: DRAFT / ARCHITECTURAL ANALYSIS (NO ACTIONS EXECUTED)\n\nCHANGE LOG (fixed from previous draft):\n- CORRECTED a claim in the original Option B analysis: setting `+i` on\n  `AGY-MASTER` itself does NOT selectively allow writes only to files, while\n  blocking directory rename. In ext4, `+i` on a directory blocks ALL new\n  directory-entry operations inside it — creating, deleting, or renaming\n  any file or subdirectory directly under it. Existing subdirectories that\n  do NOT themselves have `+i` remain writable **inside themselves** (you can\n  still edit a file's contents inside `TASKS/active/`), but you cannot add or\n  remove entries in `AGY-MASTER/` itself once you've locked it.\n- This means: every subdirectory AGY needs to write NEW files into (queue\n  folders, logs, reports) MUST be created BEFORE `chattr +i` is applied to\n  the parent, and none of those specific subdirectories should themselves\n  get `+i` (only the parent + selected top-level files should).\n- Execution order across drafts 02 and 03 has been made explicit — see\n  Section 4.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE PROBLEM: EXT4 DIRECTORY RENAMING VULNERABILITY\n\nIn Linux (POSIX / ext4), the permission to rename or delete a directory\ndepends on the write permission of its **PARENT directory**, not the\ndirectory itself.\n\n- Current Parent Directory: `/home/azureuser` (Owned by `azureuser:azureuser`,\n  permission `drwxr-xr-x`)\n- Because `azureuser` has write permission in `/home/azureuser`, any process\n  running as `azureuser` can execute:\n  ```bash\n  mv /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER-DELETED\n  ```\n  even if the files inside have individual `+i` protections.\n\n---\n\n## 2. COMPARATIVE ANALYSIS: OPTION A vs. OPTION B\n\n| Dimension | Option A: Ownership `root:root` + Mode `755` | Option B: Directory-level `chattr +i` |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Mechanism** | Standard POSIX DAC (Discretionary Access Control) | Linux ext4 Inode Immutable Flag (`FS_IMMUTABLE_FL`) |\n| **Can azureuser rename `AGY-MASTER`?** | **NO** — once `azureuser` loses write on the *parent* (`/home/azureuser` would need root ownership too, which is impractical) — see caveat below. | **NO (blocked by ext4 driver itself)**. An immutable directory cannot be renamed, moved, or have entries added/removed, regardless of parent permissions. |\n| **Caveat** | Actually requires changing permissions on `/home/azureuser` too (the true parent), which affects azureuser's own home directory usability broadly. Changing only `AGY-MASTER`'s own ownership to root:root does **not** stop rename — rename permission is governed by the *containing* directory (`/home/azureuser`), not the directory being renamed. | No caveat — this correctly targets the actual vulnerable object. |\n| **Can azureuser create NEW files/subdirs directly in `AGY-MASTER/`?** | No (root-owned, mode 755, no write bit for azureuser). | No (kernel immutability blocks any new directory entry). |\n| **Can azureuser write inside EXISTING subdirectories (e.g. `TASKS/active/`)?** | Yes, if those subdirectories are still owned by azureuser. | Yes, if those subdirectories do not themselves have `+i`. |\n| **Resilience against sudo escalation** | If azureuser regains any sudo/root path, `chown` reverses this instantly. | If `chattr` is excluded from sudoers (draft 01), azureuser **cannot** remove `+i` even with a compromised process, because removing it requires `CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE` which only root has and sudo won't grant. |\n\n### Correction to the original draft's Option A row:\nThe original comparison implied Option A only requires changing `AGY-MASTER`'s\nown ownership. That is incorrect — rename/delete permission comes from the\n**parent** directory (`/home/azureuser`), so Option A would actually require\nlocking down `/home/azureuser` itself, which is invasive and affects\nazureuser's whole home directory. **This is why Option B is the practical\nrecommendation** — it acts directly on `AGY-MASTER`, not its parent.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ARCHITECTURAL RECOMMENDATION: OPTION B (Directory `+i`, Pre-Created Subdirectories)\n\n### Why Option B is superior:\n1. `chattr +i` on `/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER` completely solves the rename\n   vulnerability: the ext4 kernel driver refuses to rename, move, or delete\n   `AGY-MASTER`, and refuses to add/remove entries in it directly — regardless\n   of `/home/azureuser`'s own permissions.\n2. It requires no change to `/home/azureuser` itself, so azureuser's broader\n   home-directory usability is untouched.\n3. Combined with draft 01 (chattr removed from sudoers), azureuser has no path\n   to reverse this — not via sudo, not via direct syscall (`ioctl` with\n   `FS_IOC_SETFLAGS` requires `CAP_LINUX_IMMUTABLE`).\n\n### The operational requirement this creates:\nBecause locking `AGY-MASTER` blocks new entries directly inside it, **every\nsubdirectory AGY will ever need to write new files into must already exist\nbefore you apply `+i`**. This is why draft 03's script has been reordered —\nsee Section 4.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DRAFT COMMAND SPECIFICATION (FOR HUMAN REVIEW — DO NOT EXECUTE AUTONOMOUSLY)\n\n### Correct execution order (this matters — reversing steps 1 and 3 below\n### will lock AGY out of its own working directories):\n\n```bash\n# STEP 1 — Create ALL runtime state subdirectories AGY will ever write\n# new files into, BEFORE locking the parent. Add any other subdirectory\n# AGY's code creates at runtime to this list now — anything missed here\n# will be impossible to create later without temporarily un-immutabilizing\n# the parent (which requires sudo access to chattr, which draft 01 removes).\nmkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/TASKS/{queue,active,completed}\nmkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/INCIDENTS/{active,resolved}\nmkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/ACTIONS/{pending,approved,history}\nmkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/REPORTS/daily\nmkdir -p /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS\nchown -R azureuser:azureuser \\\n    /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/TASKS \\\n    /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/INCIDENTS \\\n    /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/ACTIONS \\\n    /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/REPORTS \\\n    /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS\n\n# STEP 2 — Lock top-level canonical architecture files individually\nsudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/AGENTS.md\nsudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/PROJECT_REGISTRY.md\nsudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/safety_levels.json\nsudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/protected_registry.json\n\n# STEP 3 — LAST: lock the AGY-MASTER directory itself.\n# Run this only after Steps 1 and 2 are fully verified complete — once this\n# runs, no new file or subdirectory can be added directly under AGY-MASTER\n# (existing subdirectories from Step 1 remain writable inside themselves).\nsudo chattr +i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER\n```\n\n### Rollback Procedure (If Maintenance Required):\nThis MUST be run by a human with direct console/SSH access — it is not\navailable to azureuser via sudo once draft 01 is applied, by design. It\nrequires either temporarily re-adding a scoped `chattr` sudo rule, or logging\nin as root directly via Azure Serial Console.\n```bash\nsudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER\nsudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/AGENTS.md\nsudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/PROJECT_REGISTRY.md\nsudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/safety_levels.json\nsudo chattr -i /home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/POLICIES/protected_registry.json\n```\n","Description":"Update 02-parent-directory-lock.md with ext4 parent permission correction and explicit ordering","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"/home/azureuser/AGY-MASTER/DRAFTS/02-parent-directory-lock.md","toolAction":"Updating 02-parent-directory-lock.md","toolSummary":"Sync 02-parent-directory-lock"}}]}
